WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.090 --> 00:00:05.680
Hey Wisecrack, Jared again.

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:10.320
Today we’re talking about one fierce lady with
a lot of names: Daenerys Stormborn of the

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:14.640
House Targaryen, Mother of Dragons, First
of Her Name, the Unburnt, Queen of the Andals

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:20.090
and the First Men, Khaleesi of the Great Grass
Sea, Breaker of Chains, and Subject of A Thousand

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:20.940
Fantasies.

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:22.380
"Take off your clothes."

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:26.619
I don’t need to tell you that Game of Thrones
is about the game for the throne, which is

00:00:26.619 --> 00:00:30.430
mostly political posturing with a few really
expensive battles.

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:34.160
But with so many characters vying for the
Iron Throne, there’s often more discussion

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:38.730
on who will win the Seven Kingdoms, rather
than on who would actually be the best at

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:39.730
ruling them.

00:00:39.730 --> 00:00:44.230
It’s pretty much impossible to do that comparison
in a single video, so right now we’ll just

00:00:44.230 --> 00:00:45.770
focus on Daenerys.

00:00:45.770 --> 00:00:51.040
Her speeches are grand and her dragons are
big, but is she actually an effective leader?

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:55.370
She left some pretty big messes in Slaver's
Bay that may cast doubt on her ability as

00:00:55.370 --> 00:00:58.600
a monarch, but people do bow to her a lot.

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:02.800
So today, we’re going to grade her Queen skills
with some help from one of philosophy’s

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:04.900
favorite antiheros, Niccolò Machiavelli.

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:09.950
Welcome to this Wisecrack Edition on the Philosophy
of Daenerys Targaryen.

00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:12.000
And of course —&nbsp;spoilers ahead.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:16.030
Machiavelli is one of the founders of political
philosophy, and he spent most of his time

00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:20.539
studying the qualities of good leadership:
the traits that make a person most capable

00:01:20.539 --> 00:01:24.509
of achieving success for not only the state,
but for themselves.

00:01:24.509 --> 00:01:28.840
He outlines these through three books: "The Prince," "The Art of War" — not that one! —

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:32.160
and "Discourses on the First Ten Books
of Titus Livy."

00:01:32.179 --> 00:01:36.120
While all these texts focus on different aspects
of being the big dog, today we’re going

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:40.640
to focus on three that they’re all pretty
consistent in valuing: liberty, the support

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:43.030
of the people, and a little something called
Virtù.

00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:45.880
Now, I know you’re probably thinking: Machiavelli?

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:46.970
Caring about liberty?

00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:49.170
Did the Wisecrack office have a gas leak, or something?

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:50.740
Well, just bear with us.

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:52.420
So, how does Daenerys stack up?

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:57.560
The Mother of Dragons is pretty famous for
sticking to her guns on just about everything —

00:01:57.560 --> 00:01:59.220
or at least pretending she does.

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:04.450
She’s guided by a strict moral compass that
says, “no slavery, no dead children, wrong

00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:06.489
is wrong, and no asking me questions.”

00:02:06.489 --> 00:02:11.170
Sounds okay so far, but it means that she
sometimes struggles to adapt when faced with

00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:16.239
situations that demand flexibility, like whether
or not to pardon someone who kills a known

00:02:16.239 --> 00:02:17.239
terrorist.

00:02:17.239 --> 00:02:20.010
Machiavelli calls this ability to adapt Virtù.

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:24.829
He builds it up as the most important trait
in a leader, but more than that, Virtù is

00:02:24.829 --> 00:02:30.180
about having the grit and fortitude to combat
the various uncontrollable circumstances that

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:33.310
roll your way — otherwise know as Fortuna.

00:02:33.310 --> 00:02:38.209
For Machiavelli, politics is not about laying
meticulous plans years in advance, or always

00:02:38.209 --> 00:02:43.040
getting exactly what you want, but dealing
with the cruel assh*le that is Fortune.

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:48.209
To quote some other assh*le from some other
century, the best laid plans of mice and men

00:02:48.209 --> 00:02:54.379
often go awry, and the ruler with Virtù is
ready to adeptly turn these misfortunes into

00:02:54.379 --> 00:02:56.900
victory — no matter what it takes.

00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:01.530
Whether she realizes it or not, Daenerys is
well-acquainted with the battle between Virtù

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:02.530
and Fortuna.

00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:07.890
Her whole childhood was one bit of bad fortune
after the next — being shuttled around to

00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:12.569
strangers, sold to a Khal against her will,
and thrust into a new world where she doesn’t

00:03:12.569 --> 00:03:13.780
speak the language.

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:17.700
Yet, she's able to adapt to the
events of Fortuna and even use them to gain

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:18.860
strength.

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:23.140
In less than a year, she turns her forced
Dothraki marriage into a powerful political

00:03:23.150 --> 00:03:28.670
position, and then turns the death of her beloved
husband into an opportunity to lead her own

00:03:28.670 --> 00:03:29.720
khalasar.

00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:33.379
But once she actually has power, she gets
weird about it.

00:03:33.379 --> 00:03:39.749
Danaerys is super uncompromising about slavery,
which is great, but her moral absolutism undermines

00:03:39.749 --> 00:03:41.110
her own goals.

00:03:41.110 --> 00:03:46.569
After conquering Yunkai and Astapor, and freeing
their slaves, she peaces out to her next project.

00:03:46.569 --> 00:03:51.209
Since she never bothers to establish any kind
of tenable power structure, they collapse

00:03:51.209 --> 00:03:54.760
and return to slavery, or similar, as soon
as she is gone.

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:57.740
"The masters have retaken both cities. Outside of Meereen,

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:00.440
the whole of Slaver's Bay has returned to the slavers."

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:05.840
Virtù means doing whatever is necessary for
the good of the society, not following your

00:04:05.849 --> 00:04:08.810
ethical tunnel vision into a very sh*tty tunnel.

00:04:08.810 --> 00:04:13.420
In Season 5, she turns down a peace offer
that would give freedom to the Yunkai slaves

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:18.280
and stop the conflict between her army in
Meereen and the Wise Masters of Yunkai.

00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:22.740
But she turns this down because she doesn’t
want to reopen the fighting pits, even for

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:25.280
freed slaves who still want to fight.

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:29.780
She’s so unwilling to negotiate outside
her moral comfort zone that she endangers

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:33.880
pretty much everyone and everything she claims
to care about.

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:39.260
In a rare moment of compromise, she allows
a freed slave to sell himself back to his master.

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:42.600
"I will allow you to sign a contract with your former master.

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:46.060
It may not cover a period lasting longer than a year."

00:04:46.060 --> 00:04:51.729
But even this compromise highlights how Danaerys
dogmatism is self-defeating: the old man was

00:04:51.729 --> 00:04:56.820
escaping the temporary shelters she had created
for freed slaves that were even worse than

00:04:56.820 --> 00:04:58.720
the houses of their former masters.

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:07.180
"I went to one of these places. The young prey on the old. Take what they want and beat us if we resist."

00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:11.460
Tyrion, on the other hand, is the very embodiment
of virtù as he leads Meereen while Daenerys

00:05:11.470 --> 00:05:12.470
is kidnapped.

00:05:12.470 --> 00:05:16.780
He negotiates with three cities to phase
out, rather than out-right abolish, slavery,

00:05:16.780 --> 00:05:20.040
so that masked assassins will stop roaming
the city.

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:21.640
"We do not support the Sons of the Harpy."

00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:24.820
Fine, fine. But you will cut it off all the same."

00:05:24.820 --> 00:05:29.900
Tyrion is not about letting his moral inclinations
get in the way of creating a stable society.

00:05:29.900 --> 00:05:35.980
"You're right. Slavery is a horror that should be ended at once. War is a horror that should be ended at once.

00:05:35.980 --> 00:05:37.980
I can't do both today."

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:39.919
Does this treaty get immediately broken?

00:05:39.919 --> 00:05:41.260
Well, it sure does.

00:05:41.260 --> 00:05:45.390
But at least he showed the ability to adapt
his strategies in the face of Fortuna.

00:05:45.390 --> 00:05:50.729
Daenerys, on the other hand, has exactly one strategy,
and it’s called, “Yell A Lot and Burn Stuff.”

00:05:50.729 --> 00:05:52.940
That’s not always a bad strategy.

00:05:52.940 --> 00:05:56.410
The good ol “yell and burn” has gotten
Daenerys out of being kidnapped, snagged her

00:05:56.410 --> 00:06:01.020
8,000 Unsullied soldiers, saved Meereen from
warships, and earned her the loyalty of the

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:03.650
Dothraki not once, but twice.

00:06:03.650 --> 00:06:08.639
Machiavelli tells us that boldness and violence
are often necessary for mastering Fortuna,

00:06:08.639 --> 00:06:11.410
and Danaerys certainly passes that part of
the test.

00:06:11.410 --> 00:06:15.669
However, this approach isn’t just a matter
of reacting to circumstance: you can’t always

00:06:15.669 --> 00:06:20.130
go around figuring out how to beat fortune,
once it’s already shit on your lawn.

00:06:20.130 --> 00:06:25.400
Instead, mastering fortuna requires foresight,
and pre-emptive action, like beating Fortuna

00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:28.030
to the punch and shitting on its lawn first.

00:06:28.030 --> 00:06:31.830
That’s not to say Daenerys always lacks
the ability to plan.

00:06:31.830 --> 00:06:36.690
After the owner of the Unsullied refuses her
offer of ships and gold, she trades her dragon

00:06:36.690 --> 00:06:40.890
for an army of Unsullied and then actually
just kills the master and takes both.

00:06:40.890 --> 00:06:45.830
It might seem underhanded and possibly evil,
but it was the only feasible way to achieve

00:06:45.830 --> 00:06:49.370
her goals and give liberty to thousands in
the process.

00:06:49.370 --> 00:06:53.210
Some of her shining moments of adapting to
circumstance are during battle.

00:06:53.210 --> 00:06:58.030
Her acquisition of the Unsullied and the subsequent
Sack of Astapor demonstrate that she can take

00:06:58.030 --> 00:07:02.689
advantage of opportunities that present themselves,
and her decision to arm the slaves of Meereen

00:07:02.689 --> 00:07:06.939
during the siege of the city is Machiavelli-approved
on multiple grounds.

00:07:06.939 --> 00:07:11.110
On one hand, it’s strategically sound since
she risks fewer of her own soldiers.

00:07:11.110 --> 00:07:15.030
But beyond that, a wise leader recognizes
that citizens will always fight for their

00:07:15.030 --> 00:07:18.810
own liberty, and one can use that to their
own advantage.

00:07:18.810 --> 00:07:19.949
More on that in a minute.

00:07:19.949 --> 00:07:24.860
With a few exceptions, Daenerys isn’t exactly
a roll-with-the-punches kinda Queen.

00:07:24.860 --> 00:07:26.800
"Where are my dragons?"

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:31.669
Not being able to adapt your moral code in
times of necessity is a recipe for trouble,

00:07:31.669 --> 00:07:32.680
as we’ve seen again

00:07:32.680 --> 00:07:33.940
and again

00:07:33.940 --> 00:07:35.020
and again.

00:07:35.020 --> 00:07:38.620
"My father, one of Meereen's most respected and beloved citizens,

00:07:38.620 --> 00:07:43.840
oversaw the restoration and maintenance of its greatest landmarks . This pyramid included."

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:47.100
"For that, he has my gratitude. I should be honored to meet him."

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:50.100
"You have, your grace. You crucified him."

00:07:50.100 --> 00:07:54.800
So, I’m gonna pull a grade out of my ass
and give her a C for virtù.

00:07:54.800 --> 00:07:56.110
Next up is Liberty.

00:07:56.110 --> 00:07:59.759
Daenerys, the Breaker of Chains— who could
be more into freedom?

00:07:59.759 --> 00:08:04.400
She bans slavery wherever she goes and holds
open court with basically every single resident

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:05.419
of Meereen.

00:08:05.419 --> 00:08:10.719
While "The Prince" was written as a how-to guide
for directing power to, well, princes, Machiavelli

00:08:10.719 --> 00:08:16.520
strongly values liberty and, despite what
you’ve heard, advocates for a vivere libero,

00:08:16.520 --> 00:08:21.510
or a constitutional republic that allows for
civil liberties and civic participation for

00:08:21.510 --> 00:08:22.510
commoners.

00:08:22.510 --> 00:08:27.250
Machiavelli writes in "Discourses on Livy" that
one should create order by facilitating communication

00:08:27.250 --> 00:08:29.560
between nobility and common people.

00:08:29.560 --> 00:08:34.090
Daenerys tells us over and over that she has
no interest in replacing the slave masters

00:08:34.090 --> 00:08:36.700
by becoming yet another diabolical overlord.

00:08:36.700 --> 00:08:39.580
Although she can never let us forget that
she is queen,

00:08:39.580 --> 00:08:41.640
"Politics is the art of compromise, your grace."

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:44.140
"I'm not a politician. I'm queen."

00:08:44.180 --> 00:08:46.100
she does seem to be genuinely

00:08:46.110 --> 00:08:49.000
concerned with the wants and needs of her
people.

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:51.280
Machiavelli’s advice is practical.

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:55.910
People will always fight for liberty, both
internally and externally, so you should probably

00:08:55.910 --> 00:08:59.610
just go ahead and give it to them and avoid
all of the drama of getting ambushed in the

00:08:59.610 --> 00:09:00.970
fighting pits and such.

00:09:00.970 --> 00:09:05.060
In Machiavelli’s vision of a free republic,
one of the most central goals is the protection

00:09:05.060 --> 00:09:08.790
of public discourse, especially in the form
of debates.

00:09:08.790 --> 00:09:12.220
Settling conflict through rhetoric means that
the people get to choose the best course of

00:09:12.220 --> 00:09:14.330
action for the well-being of the state.

00:09:14.330 --> 00:09:18.620
The idea is, it’s much harder to fool a
thousand people than it is to fool one leader.

00:09:18.620 --> 00:09:23.030
While we don’t see much civilized public
debate in the still-chaotic Meereen, Daenerys

00:09:23.030 --> 00:09:28.160
tries to show that she prioritizes discourse
by including both former slaves and the former

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:30.240
slave masters on her small council.

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:34.970
Now it’s true that she mostly ignores the advice
from the former slave masters, but who can

00:09:34.970 --> 00:09:35.970
blame her?

00:09:35.970 --> 00:09:36.970
Well, Machiavelli can.

00:09:36.970 --> 00:09:41.250
He says that the only kinds of political systems
that truly work for the good of the people

00:09:41.250 --> 00:09:46.030
are those with open discourse— discourse
between nobles and commoners, between different

00:09:46.030 --> 00:09:48.000
competing interest groups.

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:52.130
Basically everybody should be talking to everybody
else to resolve problems, or else the only

00:09:52.130 --> 00:09:53.570
option is violence.

00:09:53.570 --> 00:09:55.250
Seems obvious, right?

00:09:55.250 --> 00:10:00.010
But in Daenerys's city, there isn’t really
discourse with the old Meereenese families.

00:10:00.010 --> 00:10:04.340
She makes a show of paying attention to all
sides but never takes their concerns about

00:10:04.340 --> 00:10:06.610
culture or the economy seriously.

00:10:06.610 --> 00:10:09.500
And, surprise, this leads to violence.

00:10:09.500 --> 00:10:13.500
You could argue that it’s fine since the
majority of the city clearly wants to punish

00:10:13.500 --> 00:10:18.070
the old slave masters, but here’s where
she really screws up: she doesn’t even listen

00:10:18.070 --> 00:10:19.500
to the majority of the people.

00:10:19.500 --> 00:10:24.190
It’s not like she spares the former slave
who kills the Son of Harpy or finds better

00:10:24.190 --> 00:10:25.800
housing for the poor.

00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:29.740
But we can’t pretend that she’s doing
it all for show; her problem is that she cares

00:10:29.740 --> 00:10:32.960
more about individual petitioners than about
open debate.

00:10:32.960 --> 00:10:38.040
Daenerys's vivere libero could use some
work, but she definitely gets points for trying.

00:10:38.040 --> 00:10:40.210
We're gonna go ahead and give her a B minus.

00:10:40.210 --> 00:10:44.220
Liberty correlates pretty strongly with popular
support, and Machiavelli makes it clear that

00:10:44.220 --> 00:10:48.560
a ruler needs the people’s support in order
to get power or keep power.

00:10:48.560 --> 00:10:51.320
Heavy-handed dictatorship is usually fleeting.

00:10:51.320 --> 00:10:53.860
On the face of it, it looks like Daenerys
has this one in the bag.

00:10:53.860 --> 00:10:57.650
I mean, people cheer at her everywhere she
goes and call her “Mhysa."

00:10:57.650 --> 00:11:00.040
The freed slaves are really into Mhysa.

00:11:00.040 --> 00:11:04.240
The problem with this is that freed slaves
are not all of the people in Meereen, Yunkai,

00:11:04.240 --> 00:11:05.310
and Astapor.

00:11:05.310 --> 00:11:06.400
And the old slavers?

00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:08.120
Definitely not into Mhysa.

00:11:08.120 --> 00:11:12.173
You know the Machiavelli quote, "it’s better
to be feared than loved?" Well, it’s not good

00:11:12.173 --> 00:11:13.390
to be hated.

00:11:13.390 --> 00:11:14.790
People try to kill you.

00:11:14.790 --> 00:11:18.890
And the Sons of the Harpy try to kill Daenerys
pretty much every time that she’s occupying

00:11:18.890 --> 00:11:19.890
their city.

00:11:19.890 --> 00:11:25.950
Shocker — she swoops in on a dragon, uproots
their way of life, and refuses to negotiate.

00:11:25.950 --> 00:11:28.480
This isn’t the first time someone’s tried
to off her.

00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:33.230
King Robert tried to poison her with wine
back in Season 1 — but, then again, attacks from foreign

00:11:33.230 --> 00:11:38.230
rulers are just a day in the life; rebellion
from the people threatens the stability and

00:11:38.230 --> 00:11:40.160
legitimacy of the crown.

00:11:40.160 --> 00:11:44.230
But all those civil liberties we mentioned
earlier serve a dual purpose.

00:11:44.230 --> 00:11:48.280
Not only will the people be more likely to
support you, they’ll also be more likely

00:11:48.280 --> 00:11:50.010
to fight battles for you.

00:11:50.010 --> 00:11:53.780
That way, you get a free army of people who
care about the cause.

00:11:53.780 --> 00:11:58.390
Machiavelli explains in "The Art of War" that
mercenaries fighting for money probably won’t

00:11:58.390 --> 00:12:01.600
be excited to die for you in the way that
true believers would.

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:07.320
And, if you want to conquer the Seven Kingdoms,
you're gonna need people to die for you.

00:12:07.320 --> 00:12:08.320
Take the Unsullied.

00:12:08.320 --> 00:12:11.530
They aren’t exactly sellswords when they’re
first introduced; they’re slaves.

00:12:11.530 --> 00:12:14.160
They aren’t fighting for loyalty or religion.

00:12:14.160 --> 00:12:19.590
However, by freeing them, Daenerys has transformed
them from unwilling mercenaries to dedicated

00:12:19.590 --> 00:12:22.420
soldiers who are now devoted to her cause.

00:12:22.420 --> 00:12:26.230
So far, they’ve been her best fighters and
their leader, Grey Worm, is one of her most

00:12:26.230 --> 00:12:27.230
trusted advisors.

00:12:27.230 --> 00:12:32.400
So, while freeing the Unsullied could be just another shining symbol of Daenerys's wokeness,

00:12:32.400 --> 00:12:34.070
it's also strategic.

00:12:34.070 --> 00:12:38.200
It’s likely no accident that she leaves
the mercenaries in Meereen when she ships

00:12:38.200 --> 00:12:42.230
off to Westeros with the troops that now very
much believe in her.

00:12:42.230 --> 00:12:44.510
Something similar happens with the Dothraki
horde.

00:12:44.510 --> 00:12:49.010
Rather than buying their support the way Viserys
tried to, she earns it by burning their leaders

00:12:49.010 --> 00:12:52.970
to death and then going all exhibitionist
in front of a collapsed temple filled with

00:12:52.970 --> 00:12:54.190
smoking corpses.

00:12:54.190 --> 00:12:56.260
Hey, whatever gets the job done.

00:12:56.260 --> 00:12:59.030
But this isn’t the first time she’s lead
a group of Dothraki.

00:12:59.030 --> 00:13:02.710
In Season 2, she lead her small khalasar through the Red Waste, where the

00:13:02.710 --> 00:13:06.220
whole camp nearly died of thirst, hunger,
and exposure.

00:13:06.220 --> 00:13:08.580
That’s almost impressively incompetent.

00:13:08.580 --> 00:13:11.820
And if it happens again, I wouldn’t count on
a lot of support.

00:13:11.820 --> 00:13:16.200
It should be obvious, but Machiavelli reminds
us, you don’t get the loyalty of the people

00:13:16.200 --> 00:13:18.470
if their basic needs aren’t met.

00:13:18.470 --> 00:13:22.610
That means Daenerys needs to feed her soldiers,
but it also hearkens back to the mistake of

00:13:22.610 --> 00:13:26.080
putting up the former slaves of Meereen in
some hellish barracks.

00:13:26.080 --> 00:13:30.640
She freed tens of thousands of people in five
seconds and then took even less time than

00:13:30.640 --> 00:13:34.220
that to create an infrastructure that could
support those people.

00:13:34.220 --> 00:13:38.080
One of Daenerys’s strongest skills is getting
the support of the people.

00:13:38.080 --> 00:13:40.960
Keeping that support turns out to be a bit
more challenging.

00:13:40.960 --> 00:13:46.660
But, as of the end of Season 6, she’s sailing
across the Narrow Sea with over 100,000 people

00:13:46.660 --> 00:13:48.540
who want to put her on the throne.

00:13:48.540 --> 00:13:51.740
So, we're gonna go ahead and say she gets a B in popular support.

00:13:51.740 --> 00:13:55.690
So, here are our final thoughts on Daenerys — and we have a few.

00:13:55.690 --> 00:13:59.630
According to  "The Art of War," a leader should
pursue the common good of the state while

00:13:59.630 --> 00:14:02.020
also seeking personal glory.

00:14:02.020 --> 00:14:04.890
Daenerys mostly just has the second one down.

00:14:04.890 --> 00:14:09.311
She thinks she cares about helping the former
slaves, but obviously not that much since

00:14:09.311 --> 00:14:13.590
she did a shit job of it and mostly just
talks about how she and her Dothraki squad

00:14:13.590 --> 00:14:17.500
are going to take back the throne that’s
thousands of miles away once she’s done

00:14:17.500 --> 00:14:20.690
playing Pretend Queen on the practice fields
of Essos.

00:14:20.690 --> 00:14:24.970
And, just for good measure, let’s address
the fact that she constantly refers to Westeros

00:14:24.970 --> 00:14:26.540
as “that which is mine.”

00:14:26.540 --> 00:14:31.080
"I want them because they're mine by right. The Iron Throne is mine, and I will take it."

00:14:31.080 --> 00:14:34.960
Demanding respect simply because she is the
“rightful heir” to the Iron Throne is

00:14:34.970 --> 00:14:38.410
in her Signature Moves list right up there
with Yelling and Burning.

00:14:38.410 --> 00:14:40.700
As Machiavelli might say: “she trippin".

00:14:40.700 --> 00:14:46.360
A ruler only gets real power through taking
and earning and keeping it, not through inheritance.

00:14:46.360 --> 00:14:48.680
"Forgive me, Khaleesi, but your ancestor,

00:14:48.680 --> 00:14:52.100
Aegon the Conqueror didn't seize six of the kingdoms because they were his right —

00:14:52.100 --> 00:14:53.460
he had no right to them —

00:14:53.460 --> 00:14:54.860
he seized them because he could."

00:14:54.860 --> 00:14:59.720
And here we land at the real question — what’s
Daenerys's plan when she gets to Westeros?

00:14:59.720 --> 00:15:03.190
The Dothraki and the Unsullied will do what
they do best — and then what?

00:15:03.190 --> 00:15:07.220
Does she have a plan for feeding and housing
the thousands of Unsullied, who are technically

00:15:07.220 --> 00:15:12.160
not her slaves but have no other job training
or any other cultural knowledge of Westeros?

00:15:12.160 --> 00:15:16.900
Sure, battling Fortuna is more important than
sticking to strict policies, but you have

00:15:16.900 --> 00:15:19.210
to start with some kind of strategy.

00:15:19.210 --> 00:15:20.630
And how about the Dothraki?

00:15:20.630 --> 00:15:24.860
Maybe they’ll kill and rape and raid and
win the Seven Kingdoms, and then settle down

00:15:24.860 --> 00:15:29.150
and build some houses on newly-liberated Lannister
land and start growing tomatoes.

00:15:29.150 --> 00:15:33.240
If the Northerners are up in arms over Wildling
raids, imagine how Daenerys's new subjects

00:15:33.240 --> 00:15:38.450
will feel about 100,000 Dothraki raiders murdering
and pillaging every inch of the continent,

00:15:38.450 --> 00:15:40.280
long after the war is over.

00:15:40.280 --> 00:15:44.720
The consistent goal in all of Machiavelli’s
work is political stability, which, as we

00:15:44.720 --> 00:15:48.290
can see, is not exactly Daenerys’s strong
suit.

00:15:48.290 --> 00:15:52.190
Being a ruler isn’t just about hopping around
from city to city for personal glory.

00:15:52.190 --> 00:15:55.500
It’s making sure that things run smoothly
once they're yours.

00:15:55.500 --> 00:15:59.820
"How can I rule seven kingdoms if I can't control Slaver's Bay?"

00:15:59.820 --> 00:16:01.280
So, is Daenerys a good leader?

00:16:01.280 --> 00:16:03.530
Well, we're gonna go ahead and give her a C+.

00:16:03.530 --> 00:16:07.450
Remember, virtù is weighted the heaviest
of all factors.

00:16:07.450 --> 00:16:08.950
And she kind of sucks at it.

00:16:08.950 --> 00:16:13.440
Maybe it comes down to this — she’s really
a Khaleesi, not a Queen.

00:16:13.440 --> 00:16:18.010
For the Dothraki, battle is a way of life,
and Daenerys’s strategy of Yell and Burn

00:16:18.010 --> 00:16:19.550
Stuff is a pretty good one.

00:16:19.550 --> 00:16:22.800
And when she does show us virtù, it’s usually
in a fight.

00:16:22.800 --> 00:16:26.260
Put her on a throne, and that’s where things start to falter.

00:16:26.260 --> 00:16:28.980
"You weren't made to sit on a chair in a palace."

00:16:28.980 --> 00:16:30.080
"What was I made for?"

00:16:30.080 --> 00:16:32.420
"You're a conqueror, Daenerys Stormborn."

00:16:32.420 --> 00:16:34.520
Thanks a lot for watching, Wisecrack. Peace.

